	<tr><td style="padding: 1px 1px 0 1px;">
		<a name="msg21718"></a>
		<table width="100%" cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0">
			<tr><td class="windowbg2">
				<table width="100%" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="table-layout: fixed;">
					<tr>
						<td valign="top" width="16%" rowspan="2" style="overflow: hidden;">
							<b>CHEV</b>
							<div class="smalltext">
								Guest<br />
								<br />
								<br />
								<a href="mailto:"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/email_sm.gif" alt="Email" title="Email" border="0" /></a>
							</div>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=1446.msg21718#msg21718"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=1446.msg21718#msg21718">Re: New ignorant Stranger hip hop writer disses Blue Scholars - and no one cares</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #7 on:</b> March 21, 2007, 06:36:27 pm &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;">heres what i found most telling about the article...<br /><br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote from: article in the stranger</div><div class="quote">Rawkus is stuck in the &#039;90s, the same accusation haters have leveled against Blue Scholars, whose reverent positivism resonates less with today&#039;s Clipse-loving youth than the college-educated remains of the Rawkus bandwagon. Of course Rawkus and the Scholars have teamed up: They&#039;re identically toothless. The deal makes perfect sense.<br /><br />Amid the confusion, what&#039;s certain is that Seattle, overwhelmingly white and educated, has an inferiority complex over its hiphop scene. We&#039;ve long had a tendency to intellectualize the music, assuming &quot;real&quot; hiphop lives in NYC or elsewhere.<br /></div><br />first it tell us that the scholars  positivism &quot;resonates less with the clipse loving youth than the college eduacated remains of the rawkus bandwagon&quot;....  then goes on to say  &quot;what&#039;s certain is that Seattle, overwhelmingly white and educated&quot;... huh ?  if seattle is mostly white and educated.. then there must not be many of those &quot;clipse loving youth&quot;  than we were led to believe.    note* i feel him on that <table border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" style="display: inline; vertical-align: middle; font: inherit;"><tr><td style="filter: Glow(color=red, strength=2); font: inherit;">ny centric </td></tr></table> &nbsp;shit though..it gets tiresome after a while.. but what do you expect ?  you think rome aint gonna talk about their philosophers forever ?<br /><br />then it goes to say <br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote from: article in the stranger</div><div class="quote">That the Emerald City celebrates the Scholars and the Mass Line record label while largely ignoring its own vibrant gangsta scene, for instance, shows a prejudice toward the East Coast, true-school sound. Innovation and originality—developing a real &quot;Seattle sound&quot;—are therefore a lower priority to local artists. From KRS-One&#039;s endorsement of Common Market to the Scholars&#039; Rawkus signing, Seattle&#039;s dependence on outside validation has hurt its homegrown scene.<br /></div><br />after already saying that <br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote from: article in the stranger</div><div class="quote">Rawkus&#039;s legendary, late-&#039;90s winning streak (Mos Def, Talib Kweli, Pharoahe Monch, Company Flow) is the Scholars&#039; primary artistic influence.<br /></div><br />Well ? if the blue scholars “primary influences”  are from the east coast.. why are they being held to the choppingboard of  “developing a real seattle sound” ?  as if the seattle sound will have no influence from pioneers ? <br />And to boot, he added that “. From KRS-One&#039;s endorsement of Common Market to the Scholars&#039; Rawkus signing, Seattle&#039;s dependence on outside validation has hurt its homegrown scene.” Is he serious ?  <br />&nbsp;If he had done any research on ra, he may have noticed KRS-ONE  at the top of dudes influences and the whole album (temple of hiphop) tour. <br />And.why he gone look to ra scion for a seattle sound ? wheh ra has already stated in several articles he is from <span style="color: Red;">Kentucky</span> ?<br />He acts like the rawkus signing  (east coast attention) hurts seattle? When infact..  it gives seattle more shine in a place we often complain about not having having it.. the largest market that is known as <span style="color: Blue;">new york</span>.<br /><br />Im not saying this isnt a good opinion, but it&#039;s not good journalism.&nbsp; infact..i think it is a good opinion..because i have heard things like this before but frequenting the massline forum and beign a fan of the label artists..&nbsp; these kinds of opinions are often left out of our disscusion..thanks for sparking convo.</div>
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
